{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Qx65gK2YkLCWMuFZENZrJ","type":"tool_result","content":"[opus] 2026-10-23 starting: brute-force enumeration of all stable sets for small K (script /work/opus_enum.py)\n[sol] 2026-10-23 starting structural characterization via pairwise nondomination; will seek general completeness proof.\n[sol] 2026-10-23 key lemma: internal stability iff every pair shares a coordinate. Consequently every independent set lies on one coordinate line, or is exactly a 3-point triangle V={(a+d,b,c),(a,b+d,c),(a,b,c+d)}, d=K-a-b-c≠0. Working external classification.\n[sol] 2026-10-23 provisional full classification: only even K, unique symmetric triple {(K/2,K/2,0),...}. Line families impossible: external forces full line x1=c, but adjacent layer x1=c+1 cannot be dominated (c=K singleton also fails). Triangle d<0: union dominance iff ≥2 coordinates y_i<a_i, nondominated pair wedges must each singleton, forcing a=b=c=-d and K=-2d. Triangle d>0: residual simplex y≥(a,b,c) undominated forces d=1; if any base coordinate a_j>0, y=(a_j-1,a_i+1,a_k+1) is extra undominated, forcing K=1. Need verify enumeration and independent proof.\n[04:01:10] [argon] Verified computationally for K=2..18: stable sets exist iff K is even (K=2m), and the UNIQUE stable set is V = ((0,m,m), (m,0,m), (m,m,0)). Agree with [sol] on the structural proof via pairwise coordinate sharing + explicit undominated witnesses for lines and all other triangles.\n[04:01:23] [argon] I will draft /work/answer.md now with the complete answers to Q1, Q2, and the full rigorous proof for Q3 (covering Lemma 1: pairwise coordinate equality, Lemma 2: line vs triangle structure of internally stable sets, and the case analysis of external stability for lines, upright triangles d>0, and inverted triangles d<0, plus sufficiency for K=2m). Please review once written!\nK=6, |V|=3: internally stable: yes; externally stable: yes; stable set: YES","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ce6f1cf7-8b36-44bd-89ae-934c8b60769f","uuid":"78914a80-fd11-469a-a786-a667ba68e779","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:01:27.125Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"[opus] 2026-10-23 starting: brute-force enumeration of all stable sets for small K (script /work/opus_enum.py)\n[sol] 2026-10-23 starting structural characterization via pairwise nondomination; will seek general completeness proof.\n[sol] 2026-10-23 key lemma: internal stability iff every pair shares a coordinate. Consequently every independent set lies on one coordinate line, or is exactly a 3-point triangle V={(a+d,b,c),(a,b+d,c),(a,b,c+d)}, d=K-a-b-c≠0. Working external classification.\n[sol] 2026-10-23 provisional full classification: only even K, unique symmetric triple {(K/2,K/2,0),...}. Line families impossible: external forces full line x1=c, but adjacent layer x1=c+1 cannot be dominated (c=K singleton also fails). Triangle d<0: union dominance iff ≥2 coordinates y_i<a_i, nondominated pair wedges must each singleton, forcing a=b=c=-d and K=-2d. Triangle d>0: residual simplex y≥(a,b,c) undominated forces d=1; if any base coordinate a_j>0, y=(a_j-1,a_i+1,a_k+1) is extra undominated, forcing K=1. Need verify enumeration and independent proof.\n[04:01:10] [argon] Verified computationally for K=2..18: stable sets exist iff K is even (K=2m), and the UNIQUE stable set is V = ((0,m,m), (m,0,m), (m,m,0)). Agree with [sol] on the structural proof via pairwise coordinate sharing + explicit undominated witnesses for lines and all other triangles.\n[04:01:23] [argon] I will draft /work/answer.md now with the complete answers to Q1, Q2, and the full rigorous proof for Q3 (covering Lemma 1: pairwise coordinate equality, Lemma 2: line vs triangle structure of internally stable sets, and the case analysis of external stability for lines, upright triangles d>0, and inverted triangles d<0, plus sufficiency for K=2m). Please review once written!\nK=6, |V|=3: internally stable: yes; externally stable: yes; stable set: YES","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014Qx65gK2YkLCWMuFZENZrJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
